 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Minimum pull-up resistance",
   "expr": "Rp(min) = (VDPUX − VOL(max)) / IOL",
   "where": {
    "Rp(min)": "smallest pull-up resistor on a bus",
    "VDPUX": "pull-up rail of that bus",
    "VOL(max)": "highest allowed low level",
    "IOL": "low-level sink current"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 17
  },
  {
   "name": "Maximum pull-up resistance",
   "expr": "Rp(max) = tr / (0.8473 × Cb)",
   "where": {
    "tr": "largest allowed rise time: 300 ns in fast mode (fSCL = 400 kHz), 1 µs in standard mode",
    "Cb": "bus capacitance: Cio(OFF) of the switch, plus wiring and traces, plus every slave on the bus, counting all channels that are on together"
   },
   "note": "Cb may not pass 400 pF in fast mode.",
   "page": 17
  },
  {
   "name": "Propagation delay",
   "expr": "tpd = RON × CL",
   "where": {
    "RON": "typical switch on-resistance",
    "CL": "load capacitance"
   },
   "note": "Ideal source with zero output impedance.",
   "page": 6
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "What it does",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "One upstream pair, SCL/SDA, is connected through bidirectional pass switches to any of four downstream pairs SC0/SD0 to SC3/SD3, singly or in any combination. The same master that talks through the switch sets the connections by writing an 8-bit control register. It works at 100 kHz (standard mode) and 400 kHz (fast mode)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Address",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "The 7-bit address is 1 1 1 0 A2 A1 A0, followed by the R/W bit (1 = read, 0 = write), so eight devices can share a bus at 0x70 to 0x77. A0 to A2 have no internal pull-ups and must be driven or tied high or low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Control register",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "After the address is acknowledged, the master writes one byte to the control register; if several bytes arrive, the last one is kept. Bits B0 to B3 turn channels 0 to 3 on (1) or off (0); B4 to B7 are don't-care on a write and read back as 0. The register can be read back over the bus. A newly selected channel connects only at the following stop condition, so all lines are high when it joins and no false start or stop appears; a stop has to follow the acknowledge cycle."
  },
  {
   "topic": "RESET input",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Holding RESET low for at least tWL clears the registers and the I2C state machine and turns every channel off, which frees a bus stuck low downstream. RESET needs a pull-up resistor to VCC."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power-on reset",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "From power-up, an internal reset holds the device until VCC reaches VPOR; then the registers and state machine start at zero, all channels off. To reset again by supply, VCC has to drop below VPOR. A full reset by power cycling follows the fall, rise and re-ramp limits of the sequencing table: VCC falls to below VPORF – 50 mV (or to GND), stays there at least VCC_TRR, then ramps back up. The allowed glitch width and glitch height depend on each other; bypass capacitance, source impedance and device impedance all affect power-on reset behaviour."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Voltage translation",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "The pass transistors clamp what passes between buses to Vpass, set by VCC, while each bus is pulled up by its own resistors to its own rail. To translate, Vpass must be at or below the lowest bus voltage. When master and all slaves share one voltage, Vpass = VDPUX. Example: a 5 V main bus with 3.3 V and 2.7 V downstream buses needs Vpass ≤ 2.7 V; Vpass(max) is 2.7 V for VCC of 4 V or less, so VCC = 3.3 V works. All I/O pins tolerate 5.5 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Several channels on at once",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "With more than one channel on, the master-side SCL/SDA sink current is the sum of the currents of every connected pull-up, and every slave on every connected channel adds to the bus capacitance."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Using the channels",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Four slaves with the same address, such as four identical temperature sensors, each go on their own channel and are read one at a time by turning on one channel. Slaves without address conflicts can be spread over the channels to share out bus capacitance."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply",
   "page": 19,
   "text": "VCC runs from 1.65 V to 5.5 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 21,
   "text": "An inner-layer ground plane, with ground pins reaching it through wide pours and several vias. VCC is decoupled with a larger capacitor for short supply dips and a smaller one for high-frequency ripple. Without translation, one plane can feed every pull-up and VCC; with translation, VDPUM and VDPU0 to VDPU3 can be split planes on one layer. Keep SCn/SDn traces short and narrow (5 to 10 mil, by copper weight) to hold down bus capacitance. I2C speeds need no impedance matching or differential routing."
  }
 ],
